        "market_heading": "Why home staging matters in the Boston market",
        "market_text": "<p>Boston's inventory is old, and old rooms confuse buyers: parlor-level living rooms with fireplaces at odd angles, third-floor walk-ups with sloped ceilings, basement units that need proof they can feel bright. Home staging answers the question every Boston buyer asks — will my furniture fit? — before they book the showing.</p><p>In a market where inventory is chronically tight and well-presented listings still draw multiple offers, the listing that photographs best sets the pace for the open house.</p><p>Boston also runs on a calendar almost no other US market shares. The September 1 lease turnover concentrates an enormous share of moves into a few weeks, which pulls listing activity forward into a crowded early-summer window. Photos that are ready when the listing goes live — not a week later once a stager's truck is scheduled — are the difference between catching that window and missing it.</p>",
        "local_heading": "Staged for the Boston buyer",
        "local_text": "<p>Our designers stage to the neighbourhood: classic-contemporary blends for Back Bay and Beacon Hill, cleaner modern looks for Seaport condos, and family staging for Brookline, Newton and the suburbs.</p><p>Period rooms get particular care. A Beacon Hill parlor with original mouldings and a working fireplace should look furnished, not redecorated — the staging works around the architecture buyers are paying for rather than covering it.</p>",
